@@s001 Bipartite matching via flow
@@s002 dato:side L (things to assign),due:side R,ok:pair in the matching,spento:pair not allowed
@@s003 Allowed pairs: 1 with A or B; 2 only with A; 3 with B or C. I want the <b>maximum number of pairs</b>, each element used at most once.
@@s004 I transform it into a flow: I add a source <b>s</b> with capacity 1 towards every element of L, and a sink <b>t</b> with capacity 1 from every element of R.
@@s005 The capacities 1 on s → L and on R → t prevent using an element twice. The integer flow is read as 0/1: each unit of flow is a pair.
@@s006 Matching found: <b>1–B, 2–A, 3–C</b>. Nobody is used twice, and the value of the flow gives the answer. Cost with Ford-Fulkerson: <code>O(m·n)</code>.
@@s007 Bipartite matching via flow
@@s008 Matching the maximum number of elements of one set with elements of another, only along allowed pairs. It is transformed into a flow: each unit of flow is a pair.
@@s009 Transforming into a flow
@@s010 Given two sets <code>L</code> and <code>R</code> and the allowed pairs between them: add a source <code>s</code> with arcs of capacity 1 towards every node of <code>L</code>, orient the pairs from <code>L</code> towards <code>R</code> with capacity 1, and add a sink <code>t</code> with arcs of capacity 1 from every node of <code>R</code>.
@@s011 The max flow is the maximum matching. The capacities 1 on <code>s → L</code> and <code>R → t</code> prevent using a node twice.
@@s012 Matching: allowed pairs and flow
@@s013 The cost and the variants
@@s014 With Ford-Fulkerson: <code>O(mn)</code>. With Hopcroft-Karp: <code>O(m√n)</code>. If the limits are not 1 (an element can be used twice), only the capacity of the arc changes.
@@s015 Kőnig: in a bipartite graph the minimum vertex cover equals the maximum matching. Hall: a perfect matching exists if, for every subset of L, its neighbourhood has at least as many elements.
@@s016 The code, side by side
@@s017 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
